        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Attic Fans",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-attic-fan/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best attic fan?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you are buying one, the QuietCool AFG SMT ES-3.0 gable fan, because its variable-speed motor draws only about 23 watts on low, it has thermostat, humidistat and app control, and QuietCool publishes realistic tested airflow. For a hip roof, its roof-mount sibling, the AFR SMT ES-2.0, is the equivalent choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are attic fans worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not in an air-conditioned house. Florida Solar Energy Center testing found a conventional attic fan used more electricity than it saved in cooling, and a solar unit cut cooling energy only about 6 percent. They make more sense in unconditioned garages, workshops and homes without air conditioning, with good intake."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many CFM attic fan do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Makers size at roughly 0.6 to 0.75 CFM per square foot of attic floor. A 1,500 square foot attic points to about 1,000 to 1,100 CFM. Size to your intake vent area as well, since a fan cannot exhaust more than the soffits can supply without pulling air from the house."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do solar attic fans work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They do move air and cost nothing to run, but less than their ratings suggest. Independent testing measured solar attic fans at about 600 to 800 CFM at peak sun, well below the 1,500 CFM or more often printed on the box. Output falls on cloudy days and stops at night."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a gable fan or roof fan better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A gable fan is easier and cannot leak because it mounts behind an existing gable vent with no new roof hole. A roof-mount fan suits hip roofs and lets you place it near the ridge. Either works if the attic has enough soffit intake and no competing ridge vent nearby."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can an attic fan cause mold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can contribute. A fan pulling moist house air into a cold attic in winter, often via a humidistat, can cause condensation on the roof sheathing. In humid summers, depressurisation can draw outdoor moisture through walls. Air sealing the ceiling and switching humidistats off in winter both reduce the risk considerably."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What temperature should an attic fan turn on?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Set it high so the fan runs only on genuinely hot afternoons, commonly around 100°F or above for a hardwired fan. That limits electricity use and the time spent depressurising the house. Solar fans can run at lower settings because they draw no grid power, though the depressurisation concern still applies."
