    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most air-conditioned homes do not need a powered attic fan, and some are worse off with one, because a fan that lowers attic pressure pulls cooled air up through every gap in the ceiling. Field testing has found conventional attic fans saving less electricity than they use. But a modern low-wattage or solar fan, sized to the attic's intake vents and set to run only on the hottest days, can be a reasonable choice for the right house, and for garages and workshops it often is. Here are the fans worth buying and the checks to make before you cut a hole in the roof.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="do-you-need-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do You Need an Attic Fan at All?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before the product list, the honest answer: most air-conditioned homes do not need a powered attic fan, and some are worse off with one. Field research by the Florida Solar Energy Center measured a conventional attic fan drawing about 284 watts while cutting the cooling load by an estimated 170 watts, so it used more electricity than it saved. A later test of a solar unit, which uses no grid power, found about a 6 percent cut in cooling energy, with a payback of more than twenty years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is that an attic fan moves the wrong air when the ceiling below it leaks. It lowers the pressure in the attic, and replacement air arrives through any path it can find, including recessed lights, the attic hatch, plumbing and wiring holes and the tops of interior walls. In an air-conditioned house, that is air you have already paid to cool. Our full comparison of <a href="/whole-house-fan-vs-attic-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whole house fans vs attic fans</a> lays out the research in detail, and this guide agrees with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are still situations where a powered attic vent is reasonable. A detached garage or workshop with an unconditioned space below, an attic over a house without air conditioning, a well air-sealed ceiling with generous soffit intake, or a roofer's recommendation to replace failed powered vents on an existing system are all fair cases. So is a homeowner who wants to try one after air sealing the attic floor, with a thermostat set high enough that it only runs on the hottest afternoons.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a cooler house rather than a cooler attic, a different fan is usually the answer. In a climate with cool evenings, a whole house fan exchanges the air in the rooms you live in and can replace hours of air conditioning; see our <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best whole house fan</a> rankings. If you do buy an attic fan, the picks below are the ones we would choose, and the sections after them explain how to size and install one so it does as little harm and as much good as possible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the specification sheets rather than the box art, because attic fan airflow ratings are some of the least comparable numbers in home improvement. Most brands quote free-air CFM, measured with nothing in front of or behind the fan. A fan in a real attic pushes air through a louver or roof hood and pulls it through soffit vents, so it moves less. We gave credit to makers that publish tested figures under resistance, and to those that state how much intake vent area their fan needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Energy use was the next filter. The research against attic fans rests largely on fans drawing 250 to 300 watts to save less than that in cooling. Electronically commutated motors that run at a fraction of that power on low speed, and solar fans that draw nothing from the grid, change the arithmetic, even if they do not fix the depressurisation problem. We also looked for thermostats that can be set high, humidistats that can be switched off, and fire safety shut-offs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Durability and support mattered because attic fans live in one of the harshest places in a house, with summer attic temperatures that can exceed 140°F, and a failed fan on a roof means another trip up a ladder. We favoured brushless or ECM motors, steel housings, and warranties measured in years rather than months. We left out cheap no-name solar fans with vague specifications and short warranties.
          </p>

          <p>
            All six picks are current models from QuietCool, Master Flow, iLIVING and Remington Solar, sold through mainstream retailers. Ratings and review counts are Amazon figures at the time of writing, rounded down, and where a listing showed only a small number of ratings we used a conservative count. We have not inflated anyone's airflow: where a maker's rating looks optimistic, the product description says so.
          </p>
        </div>

