        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Roof, Gable, Solar and Smart Fans Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gable fan mounts inside the attic behind an existing gable-end vent and blows out through it. There is no new hole in the roof and nothing to flash, which makes it the easiest type to install and the least likely to leak. It needs a gable vent large enough to pass its airflow, and it works best when intake comes from soffits on the other side of the attic. On a hip roof without gable ends, it is not an option.
          </p>

          <p>
            A roof-mount fan sits in a hole cut in the roof deck near the ridge, under a weatherproof hood, and exhausts straight up. It can be placed where it does the most good, but every roof penetration is a potential leak, so flashing and shingling matter. Roof mounts are the default on hip roofs and are what most roofers install when replacing old powered vents.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Power</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Install</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch out for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gable mount</td>
